{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QcFFGVLkYK1Wswqveshdji","type":"tool_result","content":"    38→\n    39→### What gets stripped before public release\n    40→\n    41→The sync process (`scripts/sync-public.ts`) removes internal-only content:\n    42→\n    43→- **Directories**: `intermediates/`, `external-docs/`, `public-release-files/`, `.github/actions/`, `docs/`\n    44→- **Files**: `CLAUDE.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, `PUBLISHING-PLAN.md`, `telemetry.md` (root), `reference/telemetry.md` (internal version), `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, `.github/workflows/sync-public.yml`, `scripts/sync-public.ts`, `scripts/test-sync-local.ts`, `scripts/release.ts`, `.DS_Store`\n    45→\n    46→The `public-release-files/` directory contains replacements that get overlaid: a public-facing `README.md`, `LICENSE.md`, `NOTICE.md`, a sanitized `reference/telemetry.md`, and a simplified `.github/workflows/release.yml`.\n    47→\n    48→The `package.json` is transformed: name becomes `hankweave`, `publishConfig.access` becomes `public`, `homepage` points to the public repo, `release:*` scripts are removed, and the `workspaces` field is deleted (since docs are not included in the public repo).\n    49→\n    50→---\n    51→\n    52→## 2. Branch Model\n    53→\n    54→```\n    55→feature branches → develop → release/alpha\n    56→                      ↑\n    57→              all PRs target here\n    58→```\n    59→\n    60→- **`develop`** — active development. All PRs target this branch. All work starts here.\n    61→- **`release/alpha`** — the default branch on GitHub. Customer-facing. Tagged releases are cut from here. Never commit directly to release/alpha; it receives merges from develop only during the release process.\n    62→- **No `main` or `master` branch.** This is intentional.\n    63→\n    64→### Feature branch naming\n    65→\n    66→- Author-prefixed: `hrishi/sentinel-qol-fixes`, `hrishi/init-wizard`\n    67→- Descriptive: `fix/loop-iteration-telemetry`, `shim-timeout`\n    68→- Ticket-based: `ENG-90`\n    69→\n    70→The `hrishi/*` prefix is Hrishi's convention for easier identification. Philip uses bare names. Either is fine.\n    71→\n    72→---\n    73→\n    74→## 3. Development Workflow\n    75→\n    76→### The four-eyes principle\n    77→\n    78→We operate on a **four-eyes principle** (ideally six, counting agents). The workflow for non-trivial changes:\n    79→\n    80→1. **Plan** — discuss the approach, often with an AI agent. Write a plan in `intermediates/<number>-<name>/plan.md` for larger features.\n    81→2. **Review the plan** — read it critically. Replan if needed.\n    82→3. **Implement** — have an agent write the code, or write it yourself.\n    83→4. **Lint and test locally** — `bun run lint`, `bun run tc`, then tests (see testing order below).\n    84→5. **Review the code line by line** — the author reads every line before pushing.\n    85→6. **Push as a PR** — target `develop`. Include ticket numbers in the title.\n    86→7. **Peer review** — another team member reviews before merging (~90% of the time, depending on size and risk).\n    87→8. **Squash merge** — standard merge method. Keeps develop history clean.\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"72b4944f-9ed8-419f-b1b0-aa5790b55960","uuid":"53445a3a-bca1-4d2d-a268-3eaa47b76189","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md","content":"\n### What gets stripped before public release\n\nThe sync process (`scripts/sync-public.ts`) removes internal-only content:\n\n- **Directories**: `intermediates/`, `external-docs/`, `public-release-files/`, `.github/actions/`, `docs/`\n- **Files**: `CLAUDE.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, `PUBLISHING-PLAN.md`, `telemetry.md` (root), `reference/telemetry.md` (internal version), `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, `.github/workflows/sync-public.yml`, `scripts/sync-public.ts`, `scripts/test-sync-local.ts`, `scripts/release.ts`, `.DS_Store`\n\nThe `public-release-files/` directory contains replacements that get overlaid: a public-facing `README.md`, `LICENSE.md`, `NOTICE.md`, a sanitized `reference/telemetry.md`, and a simplified `.github/workflows/release.yml`.\n\nThe `package.json` is transformed: name becomes `hankweave`, `publishConfig.access` becomes `public`, `homepage` points to the public repo, `release:*` scripts are removed, and the `workspaces` field is deleted (since docs are not included in the public repo).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Branch Model\n\n```\nfeature branches → develop → release/alpha\n                      ↑\n              all PRs target here\n```\n\n- **`develop`** — active development. All PRs target this branch. All work starts here.\n- **`release/alpha`** — the default branch on GitHub. Customer-facing. Tagged releases are cut from here. Never commit directly to release/alpha; it receives merges from develop only during the release process.\n- **No `main` or `master` branch.** This is intentional.\n\n### Feature branch naming\n\n- Author-prefixed: `hrishi/sentinel-qol-fixes`, `hrishi/init-wizard`\n- Descriptive: `fix/loop-iteration-telemetry`, `shim-timeout`\n- Ticket-based: `ENG-90`\n\nThe `hrishi/*` prefix is Hrishi's convention for easier identification. Philip uses bare names. Either is fine.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Development Workflow\n\n### The four-eyes principle\n\nWe operate on a **four-eyes principle** (ideally six, counting agents). The workflow for non-trivial changes:\n\n1. **Plan** — discuss the approach, often with an AI agent. Write a plan in `intermediates/<number>-<name>/plan.md` for larger features.\n2. **Review the plan** — read it critically. Replan if needed.\n3. **Implement** — have an agent write the code, or write it yourself.\n4. **Lint and test locally** — `bun run lint`, `bun run tc`, then tests (see testing order below).\n5. **Review the code line by line** — the author reads every line before pushing.\n6. **Push as a PR** — target `develop`. Include ticket numbers in the title.\n7. **Peer review** — another team member reviews before merging (~90% of the time, depending on size and risk).\n8. **Squash merge** — standard merge method. Keeps develop history clean.","numLines":50,"startLine":38,"totalLines":429}},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T13:58:46.745Z"}
